============================================================
QDISC (QUEUEING DISCIPLINE) — COMPLETE DETAILED EXPLANATION
============================================================


============================================================
1) WHAT IS QDISC?
============================================================

--- Definition
    qdisc (Queueing Discipline) is the Linux kernel's
    SOFTWARE TRANSMIT SCHEDULER.

--- What it provides
    - Packet buffering
    - Packet scheduling
    - Traffic shaping
    - Fairness between flows
    - Backpressure handling between kernel and NIC driver

--- Layer position
    - Below Layer 3 (IP)
    - Above NIC driver
    - Often called "Layer 2.5"

--- Important clarification
    qdisc is NOT a single algorithm.
    qdisc is a FRAMEWORK that supports multiple algorithms.


============================================================
2) WHY QDISC EXISTS
============================================================

--- Problem without qdisc
    - Applications can generate packets faster than NIC can send
    - Hardware DMA rings are small and finite
    - Driver should not block or sleep
    - No fairness between flows
    - No traffic shaping or policy enforcement

--- Solution
    Insert a SOFTWARE QUEUE + SCHEDULER
    between IP and the driver.

--- That solution is qdisc.


============================================================
3) WHERE QDISC SITS IN THE TX FLOW
============================================================

--- Full TX call path
    Application
        ↓
    TCP / UDP (Layer 4)
        ↓
    IP (Layer 3)
        ↓
    ip_local_out()
        ↓
    ip_output()
        ↓
    dev_queue_xmit()
        ↓
    qdisc_enqueue()          ← qdisc begins
        ↓
    qdisc_dequeue()
        ↓
    ndo_start_xmit()         ← NIC driver
        ↓
    DMA TX ring
        ↓
    NIC hardware


============================================================
4) WHAT QDISC DOES INTERNALLY
============================================================

--- 4.1 Packet buffering
    - Stores skbs in software queues
    - Prevents packet drops when NIC is busy
    - Acts as shock absorber between kernel and hardware

--- 4.2 Packet scheduling
    - Decides which packet goes next
    - May reorder packets based on policy
    - Examples:
        * FIFO
        * Round-robin
        * Priority-based
        * Fair sharing

--- 4.3 Traffic shaping
    - Rate limiting
    - Bandwidth control
    - Artificial delays

--- 4.4 Traffic policing
    - Drop packets exceeding policy
    - Enforce QoS rules

--- 4.5 Flow control integration
    - Honors netif_tx_stop_queue()
    - Stops dequeuing when driver is busy
    - Resumes when netif_tx_wake_queue() is called


============================================================
5) WHAT HAPPENS WHEN TX QUEUE IS STOPPED
============================================================

--- Condition
    Driver calls:
        netif_tx_stop_queue(txq)

--- Effect inside qdisc
    - qdisc_enqueue() continues to accept skbs
    - qdisc_dequeue() stops sending skbs to driver
    - ndo_start_xmit() is NOT called

--- Result
    - skb remains queued inside qdisc
    - skb is NOT returned to TCP
    - skb is NOT stored in sk_write_queue
    - Layer 4 is completely unaware


============================================================
6) PACKET STATE INSIDE QDISC
============================================================

--- skb contents
    - Ethernet header present
    - IP header present
    - TCP / UDP header present
    - Payload present

--- Processing already done
    - Routing decision complete
    - Neighbor resolution complete
    - Netfilter POST_ROUTING complete

--- Missing step
    - Submission to NIC driver via ndo_start_xmit()


============================================================
7) QDISC TYPES (IMPLEMENTATIONS)
============================================================

--- Common qdiscs
    pfifo_fast
    fq
    fq_codel
    tbf
    netem
    mq

--- Important point
    ALL of the above are qdisc IMPLEMENTATIONS,
    NOT replacements for qdisc itself.


============================================================
8) FQ (FAIR QUEUEING) QDISC
============================================================

--- What fq is
    fq is a SPECIFIC qdisc implementation.

--- What fq solves
    - Prevents single flow domination
    - Provides per-flow fairness
    - Reduces latency under load

--- Key idea
    One queue per flow instead of one global queue.


============================================================
9) HOW FQ WORKS INTERNALLY
============================================================

--- Flow classification
    Each skb is hashed using:
        - Source IP
        - Destination IP
        - Source port
        - Destination port
        - Protocol

--- Data structure
    flow_hash → per-flow queue

--- Enqueue path
    fq_enqueue():
        - Identify flow
        - Enqueue skb into that flow's queue

--- Dequeue path
    fq_dequeue():
        - Pick next active flow (round-robin)
        - Dequeue ONE skb
        - Send skb to ndo_start_xmit()


============================================================
10) WHY FQ IS BETTER THAN PFIFO_FAST
============================================================

--- pfifo_fast problems
    - Single global queue
    - Greedy flow can starve others
    - High latency (bufferbloat)

--- fq advantages
    - Per-flow isolation
    - Fair bandwidth sharing
    - Lower latency
    - Better interactive performance


============================================================
11) FQ VS FQ_CODEL
============================================================

--- fq
    - Fair queueing
    - No active queue management
    - Simple drop logic

--- fq_codel
    - Fair queueing
    - CoDel AQM (latency control)
    - Smarter drop decisions

--- Default choice
    Modern Linux uses fq_codel by default


============================================================
12) IMPORTANT RELATIONSHIPS (DO NOT CONFUSE)
============================================================

--- qdisc
    Framework / infrastructure

--- fq
    One qdisc implementation

--- netdev_tx_queue
    Driver-facing software queue state

--- DMA TX ring
    Hardware descriptor queue


============================================================
13) ONE-LINE MENTAL MODEL
============================================================

    qdisc is the TRAFFIC CONTROLLER
    fq is ONE POLICY used by that controller


============================================================
14) FINAL SUMMARY
============================================================

- qdisc is mandatory for TX scheduling
- fq is NOT a replacement for qdisc
- fq IS a qdisc implementation
- qdisc buffers packets and applies policy
- fq ensures fairness between flows
- Driver only sees packets AFTER qdisc
- TX queue stop affects qdisc, not TCP

